{"parentUuid":"4c095c29-c8bb-4450-a523-ca9376bd7203","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RxTnMHQePCTJQrMd1pbZam","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ck8LG4esV46P6KasjNUK6b","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nSpeed up entire clean --all and add progress + escape hatch\n\n`entire clean --all` enumerates archived v2 generations as part of the\npreview. The previous flow blocked silently — sometimes for many seconds\nor indefinitely — before any output appeared, and Ctrl-C didn't reliably\nescape because go-git's tree iteration ignores context cancellation.\n\nFour changes:\n\n1. Print \"Checking remote for archived v2 generations...\" immediately\n   when v2 is enabled, then run the v2 enumeration in a goroutine\n   concurrent with the fast local enumeration. The user sees feedback\n   right away instead of staring at a blank terminal.\n\n2. Emit per-generation progress (\"scanning archived generation 3/18\n   (<name>)\") so a long run reads as making progress, not hung.\n\n3. Add a `ctx.Err()` check between candidates so SIGINT can exit\n   cleanly between generations, and add a second-SIGINT force-exit\n   handler in main.go: the first Ctrl-C cancels the context, a second\n   Ctrl-C calls os.Exit(130). This works for any future hang where a\n   library doesn't honor cancellation, not just this one.\n\n4. *** Trade-off: trust generation.json over raw transcripts. ***\n   The hot path was walking every archived checkpoint's full-tree via\n   go-git and parsing each transcript JSONL to derive timestamps.\n   That's where the multi-second-per-generation hang came from. The\n   archived /full/ refs intentionally don't carry per-session\n   metadata.json (the split is: /main has metadata.json + prompts,\n   /full has transcripts only), so generation.json at the tree root\n   was the only cheap source — and the prior code consulted it only\n   as a fallback when the tree walk found no timestamps.\n\n   This commit inverts the preference: read generation.json first; fall\n   back to the tree walk only when generation.json is missing or\n   incomplete. generation.json is written at archival time and\n   actively maintained by generation_repair.go, so in practice it is\n   authoritative.\n\n   What changes in behavior:\n   - When generation.json AGREES with the raw transcripts (the normal\n     case): no observable difference, except the operation completes\n     orders of magnitude faster.\n   - When generation.json is STALE OR WRONG and says \"newer than\n     actual\": we'd skip cleanup for a generation that's actually\n     eligible — i.e. we leave data on disk longer than retention. Safe\n     direction.\n   - When generation.json is STALE OR WRONG and says \"older than\n     actual\": we'd delete a generation that isn't actually past\n     retention yet. Unsafe direction. Mitigation: generation_repair.go\n     already exists to keep generation.json correct.\n\n   The test that previously pinned the opposite preference\n   (UsesRawTranscriptTimeForV2GenerationRetention) is renamed to\n   PrefersGenerationJSONOverRawTranscript and its assertion is flipped\n   to document the new contract.\n\nThe first three changes are pure UX/correctness improvements with no\ntrade-off. The fourth is the actual speedup and carries the trade-off\ndocumented above; it should be revisited if a user reports data\ndeleted prematurely due to a wrong generation.json.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\n)\"","description":"Commit with detailed message"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":502,"cache_read_input_tokens":226506,"output_tokens":1226,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":502,"ephemeral_5