ion. This comment belongs in cleanup.go (which already has it, lines 384-389 in the patched file), not duplicated in the test.\n\n**Action:** Shorten the test comment to: \"Cleanup prefers generation.json timestamps over computed transcript ages.\" Remove the design justification (tree walk cost, generation_repair.go, etc.)—that's in cleanup.go.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Second SIGTERM Handler Comment Redundancy** (cmd/entire/main.go:191-194)\nThe comment is well-intentioned but repeats what the code shows: \"Second signal forces exit... give the user an escape hatch.\" The \"Some long-running operations... don't honor context cancellation\" detail is valuable, but \"escape hatch\" is narrative.\n\n**Action:** Reword to: \"Some long-running operations (e.g. go-git tree walks) don't honor context cancellation, so a second signal provides a forced exit.\" Remove \"escape hatch.\"\n\n---\n\n**Summary:** No critical bugs. Issues are mainly around explicitness (goroutine state ownership, nested OR condition clarity) and comment hygiene (removing narrative, avoiding duplication with cleanup.go). The code is functionally sound and the concurrent enumeration is a sensible optimization.</result>\n<usage><total_tokens>26373</total_tokens><tool_uses>11</tool_uses><duration_ms>30669</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
