optional writer, but document why progress is treated differently from w/errW pairs.\n\n**Action:** Add a comment explaining the single-writer design choice, or migrate to a consistent options pattern when the next parameter is needed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Leaky Abstraction: Progress Printing in Strategy Package** (cmd/entire/cli/strategy/cleanup.go:375-377)\nThe strategy package now performs user-facing I/O (progress printing). Other functions in the package accept `io.Writer` parameters (Reset, Rewind, CheckAndWarnHookManagers), but `ListEligibleV2Generations` is the first to print progress autonomously. This works but creates an implicit contract: callers must pass `cmd.ErrOrStderr()` or equivalent. If a caller forgets to pass a writer (nil), progress silently vanishes; no error signals this configuration choice.\n\n**Action:** Document in the function comment whether nil progress is a valid/expected configuration (it is, per line 375 guard), and consider naming it more explicitly if it's optional (e.g., `progressWriter` instead of `progress`).\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Nested Condition Complexity** (cmd/entire/cli/strategy/cleanup.go:391)\nThe condition `if genErr != nil || gen.OldestCheckpointAt.IsZero() || gen.NewestCheckpointAt.IsZero()` groups three distinct concerns: (a) read failed, (b) timestamps missing from result. If genErr is nil but timestamps are zero, we still fallback—this is intentional per the comment but the three-way OR makes the logic harder to trace. \n\n**Action:** Add a comment before line 391 clarifying: \"Fallback to tree walk if generation.json is missing (genErr != nil) or contains incomplete timestamps.\" This justifies the compound condition.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Unnecessary Comment on Test (cmd/entire/cli/clean_test.go:74-81)**\nThe test comment is overly detailed—it explains the entire design decision (\"generation.json is authoritative, tree walks are expensive\") rather than documenting the test's specific assertion. This comment belongs in